From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:39:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09818/11362] Trace the 1822 writer: it is /bin/sh, and the
 hole is not closable by a shim

Operator directive: trace who wrote results/experiment_1822_rtl_synth.log.
Answer: the shell, performing a redirect, before yosys starts.

Makefile:52 is the ONLY reference to this path in the repository:

  yosys -p "synth_xilinx ..." rtl/potts_machine_v2.v > results/experiment_1822_rtl_synth.log

The path is a shell redirection target and is never passed to yosys. That is why
searching for `yosys -l <logfile>` call sites finds only gatemate_ising_n16*.log,
and why nothing under scripts/, python/ or tests/ names this log -- the only
mention lives in the Makefile.

Traced with strace -f -e trace=openat,execve on `make synth-constraints`, run
with the exact environment the guard's wrapped Popen passes:

  line 19: 2874708 execve("/bin/sh", ["-c", "yosys ... > results/..."])
  line 34: 2874711 openat("results/experiment_1822_rtl_synth.log",
                          O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC) = 3
  line 35: 2874711 execve("/opt/oss-cad-suite/bin/yosys") = 0

The open precedes the exec. PID 2874711 is still a forked /bin/sh at that point,
setting up `>`; yosys inherits an already-truncated fd 1. So the evidence is
destroyed by the shell, before the synthesis tool runs at all.

The guard was armed and reached the child. make received 62 vars including the
three the guard injects, children received 65 after make added its own. After the
run the redirect root was EMPTY and the tracked file had moved 3df4a636 ->
c107d1ca. The guard redirected nothing, because /bin/sh does not read PYTHONPATH
and does not import Python modules.

Classification: the first blind spot the commit already lists, non-Python
children, narrowed to its exact form -- the opener is the shell, not the tool.
No fourth unnamed hole here.

Closable? Not by this mechanism. A sitecustomize on PYTHONPATH reaches only
interpreters that import site. An LD_PRELOAD interposer on open/openat would
reach /bin/sh, and is rejected as disproportionate: it would sit in the path of
every child the suite spawns, and it fails on static and setuid binaries. The
proportionate fix is at the test and the Makefile -- add RESULTS_DIR ?= results,
use $(RESULTS_DIR) in the recipe, and have the test pass RESULTS_DIR=<tmp_path>.
That removes the write rather than intercepting it, which is the conclusion the
guard's own commit reaches in its closing line.

Corrects section 3 of this note, which said "make and yosys are compiled
binaries". The compiled binaries are not the opener. Classification and
conclusion are unchanged; the attribution is now exact. Appended rather than
rewritten, per the never-prune rule.

The file was dirtied twice by this review -- once by the guard-active pytest
reproduction, once by this strace run -- and restored both times with git
checkout, verified byte-identical at 3df4a636. --gate returns OK and results/ is
clean.

